{"indexedPages":9,"totalPages":9,"version":"753","text":"Counterfactual Reasoning for Responsibility Attribution in Probabilistic Multi-Agent Systems\nChunyan Mu1 , Muhammad Najib2\n1University of Aberdeen 2Heriot-Watt University\n{Chunyan.Mu@abdn.ac.uk, M.Najib@hw.ac.uk}\nAbstract\nResponsibility allocation—determining the extent to which agents causally contribute to outcomes— is a fundamental challenge in the design and analysis of multi-agent systems. In this work, we model such systems as concurrent stochastic multiplayer games and introduce a notion of retrospective (backward) counterfactual responsibility, which quantifies an agent’s causal contribution to outcomes resulting from a given strategy profile. To allocate responsibility among agents, we utilise the Shapley value and formally show that this method satisfies key desirable properties, including fairness and consistency. Building on this foundation, we propose a formal framework that supports both verification and strategic reasoning in responsibilityaware multi-agent systems. Furthermore, by adopting Nash equilibrium as the solution concept, we demonstrate how to compute stable strategy profiles in which agents trade off responsibility against expected reward.\n1 Introduction\nThe growing emphasis on trustworthy AI [Chatila et al., 2021; Li et al., 2023] necessitates a paradigm shift, particularly regarding responsibility and accountability [Dignum, 2020; Kaur et al., 2022; Belle et al., 2024]. To enable responsible behaviour in agents, they must be endowed with an awareness of responsibility. This motivated the notion of responsibility-aware agents [Yazdanpanah et al., 2023; Kobayashi et al., 2023], which are assumed to consider not only their own rewards/payoffs but also the broader implications of their actions. In MAS setting, where agents interact and may collaborate, the problem of responsibility attribution becomes increasingly important. When multiple agents jointly bring about an outcome, how can we fairly attribute responsibility to each agent, taking into account their respective capability? To illustrate this challenge, consider the following example.\nExample 1. Consider two autonomous vehicles, A1 and A2, approaching an uncontrolled junction under snowy conditions. A1 is travelling from east to west, A2 from south to\nnorth. Each vehicle can either brake or not brake. If neither brakes, they will crash into each other. If both successfully brake (and stop), they will avoid a collision. However, due to the slippery road conditions, there is a 0.2 chance that A1 will not be able to stop, and a 0.6 chance that A2 will not be able to stop. Each vehicle aims to minimise its travel time to reach its destination. However, a crash would result in a significant cost.\nSuppose the outcome is a collision resulting from the agents’ chosen actions (or strategies), which vehicle should bear greater responsibility for the collision? Naturally, this depends on the strategies adopted during the interaction. However, responsibility may also hinge on the agents’ capabilities [Gerstenberg et al., 2011]. To illustrate, suppose that A2 can guarantee stopping with certainty. If both agents adopt the same mixed strategy that includes a non-zero probability of not braking, and a collision occurs, it seems reasonable to assign greater responsibility to A2, as it had the capability to avoid the collision entirely. Moreover, there is an inherent trade-off between minimising travel time and avoiding collisions. This creates a strategic decision-making problem in which agents must weigh their utility (e.g., travel time) against potential responsibility penalties for a collision. Given these considerations, some important questions arise: Given the strategy profiles and capabilities, how should responsibility be allocated?, If the agents are aware of responsibility attribution, how should they choose their strategies? In this paper, we aim to answer such questions. First, we study responsibility attribution through the lens of counterfactual reasoning, which evaluates an agent’s contribution by asking how the outcome would change if the agent had acted differently. We follow well-established accounts of causation in philosophy and AI, where counterfactuals capture “what would have happened otherwise” [Chockler and Halpern, 2004; Lewis, 2013; Pearl, 2009]. We then examine strategic reasoning for responsibility-aware agents—agents that weigh not only their expected rewards but also the potential responsibility they may incur. This perspective provides a framework for addressing the earlier question of (strategic) forward reasoning: how such agents should choose their strategies.\nContribution Our main contribution is an approach for responsibility attribution in probabilistic multi-agent systems.\nProceedings of the Thirty-Fifth International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI-26)\n288\n概率多智能体系统中责任归因的反事实推理\n穆春燕1 , 穆罕默德·纳吉布2\n1阿伯丁大学2赫瑞‐瓦特\n大学 {Chunyan.Mu@abdn.ac.uk, M.Najib@hw.ac.uk}\n摘要\n责任分配——确定智能体在因果上对结果贡献的 程度——是多智能体系统设计与分析中的一项基 本挑战。在这项工作中,我们将此类系统建模为 并发随机多玩家博弈,并引入回顾性(后向)反 事实责任的概念,该概念量化了在给定策略组合 下智能体对结果的因果贡献。为了在智能体之间 分配责任,我们采用 Shapley 值,并正式证明该 方法满足若干关键理想性质,包括公平性和一致 性。在此基础上,我们提出一个形式化框架,该 框架同时支持责任感知多智能体系统中的验证与 策略推理。此外,通过采用纳什均衡作为解概念, 我们展示了如何计算稳定的策略组合,在这些组 合中,智能体在责任与期望回报之间进行权衡。\n1 引言\n对可信 AI 日益增长的重视 [Chatila et al., 2021; Li et al., 2023] 要求进行范式转变,尤其是在责任与问责方面 [ Dignum, 2020; Kaur et al., 2022; Belle et al., 2024]。 为使智能体能够表现出负责任的行为,必须赋予它们责任 意识。这催生了责任感知型智能体这一概念 [ Yazdanpanah et al., 2023; Kobayashi et al., 2023], 这 类智能体被假定不仅考虑自身的奖励/收益,还考虑其行动 的更广泛影响。在多智能体系统(MAS)场景中,智能体 相互交互并可能开展协作,责任归因问题变得日益重要。 当多个智能体共同导致某一结果时,我们如何在考虑各智 能体各自能力的情况下,公平地将责任归因于每个智能体? 为说明这一挑战,请考虑以下示例。\n例 1。考虑两辆自动驾驶车辆, A1 和 A2,在降雪条件下驶 近一个无控制交叉口。 A1 正由东向西行驶, A2 从南到\n北。每辆车都可以选择刹车或不刹车。如果两车都不刹车,它们 将相撞。如果两车都成功 刹车(并停下),它们就能避免碰撞。然而,由于路面湿 滑,有 0.2 的概率 A1 无法停车,有 0.6 的概率 A2 无法 停车。每辆车都旨在尽可能缩短到达目的地的行驶时间。 然而,发生碰撞会导致重大代价。\n假设结果是由智能体所选动作(或策略)导致的一次 碰撞,那么哪辆车应对该碰撞承担更大的责任?自然,这 取决于交互过程中所采用的策略。然而,责任也可能取决 于智能体的能力 [Gerstenberg 等, 2011]。举例来说,假 设 A2 能够确保必定停下。如果两个智能体采用相同的混 合策略,且该策略包含不刹车的概率非零,并且发生了碰 撞,那么将更大责任归于 A2似乎更合理,因为它有能力完 全避免碰撞。此外,在最小化行驶时间与避免碰撞之间存 在固有的权衡。这构成了一个策略性决策问题,智能体必 须在自身效用(例如行驶时间)与碰撞可能带来的责任惩 罚之间进行权衡。鉴于这些考虑,一些重要问题随之产生: 在给定策略组合与能力的情况下,应如何分配责任?如果 智能体知晓责任归因,它们应如何选择策略?\n在本文中,我们旨在回答这类问题。首先,我们从反 事实推理的视角研究责任归因。反事实推理通过考察如果 智能体采取不同的行动,结果会如何变化,来评估智能体 的贡献。我们遵循哲学与人工智能领域中关于因果关系的 成熟论述,其中反事实刻画了“否则会发生什么” [ Chockler and Halpern, 2004; Lewis, 2013; Pearl, 2009]。 然后,我们考察责任感知型智能体的策略推理——这类智能 体不仅权衡其预期奖励,还权衡其可能承担的潜在责任。 这一视角为解决先前关于(策略性)前向推理的问题提供 了一个框架:这类智能体应如何选择其策略。\n贡献:我们的主要贡献是提出一种用于概率多智能体系统中 责任归因的方法。\n第三十五届国际人工智能联合会议(IJCAI‐26)论文集\n288\n\n\n Firstly, we introduce a formal framework based on the Shapley value [Shapley, 1953] and show that the resulting allocations satisfy desirable properties such as fairness (responsibility reflects contribution) and consistency (an agent’s share does not decrease when its influence increase). Although we do not claim this to be a definitive account of responsibility, the framework is interpretable, and based on a concept used widely across disciplines. Our focus is not on formalising moral responsibility, an extremely complex area with no clear consensus, nevertheless, we believe that our approach can contribute toward such a formalisation.\nSecondly, we study strategic reasoning in the setting where agents employ randomised memoryless strategies (aka behavioural [Cristau et al., 2010]), i.e., functions that map from state to a probability distribution over actions. Following the tradition of formal methods in MAS, we extend the logic PATL with cumulative reward (similar to rPATL [Kwiatkowska et al., 2019]) and introduce degree of responsibility operators. We show that model checking this logic remains in PSPACE, thus no harder than rPATL [Kwiatkowska et al., 2021]. Finally, we present a method for computing Nash equilibrium strategy profiles where agents’ utility functions incorporate both reward and responsibility allocation. We show that when agents’ temporal objectives are specified as either reachability or safety conditions, these equilibria can also be computed in PSPACE. Due to space constraints, extended version of the paper containing full proofs is provided in [Mu and Najib, 2026].\nRelated work Research on responsibility and attribution spans a large body of work; here, we highlight some relevant studies and key distinctions. Responsibility attribution is closely tied to causality and counterfactual reasoning. Foundational work by Halpern and Pearl [Halpern and Pearl, 2005] introduced structural causal models, which underpin formal definitions of causality and counterfactual dependence. Building on this, [Chockler and Halpern, 2004] proposed a quantitative notion of responsibility, which later extended to a notion of blame attribution [Halpern and Kleiman-Weiner, 2018]. While conceptually related to our approach, their framework relies on directed acyclic graphs and is primarily suited for reasoning about beliefs, intentions, and epistemic states, whereas our work focuses on temporally extended properties in strategic settings. The Shapley value has also been applied for responsibility allocation. [Friedenberg and Halpern, 2019] extends the structural-model approach [Halpern and Kleiman-Weiner, 2018] by incorporating the Shapley value to distribute blame, and demonstrating that the allocation scheme satisfies desirable properties. As previously discussed, their model differs from ours and is suited for different purposes. Similarly, [Yazdanpanah et al., 2019] apply Shapley-based responsibility allocation in concurrent epistemic games, enabling epistemic modeling but under deterministic assumptions, in contrast to our probabilistic setting. [Baier et al., 2021a] studies backward and forward responsibility in extensiveform games, while [Baier et al., 2021b] employs parametric Markov chains (pMCs), which are closely related to our model. Memoryless strategy profiles naturally correspond\nto parameters assignements in a pMC, although the converse does not always hold1. Furthermore, [Baier et al., 2021b] primarily focuses on attributing changes to parameters, with its main concern being how variations in temporal satisfaction degrees should be attributed to changes in parameter assignments. In contrast, our work assigns responsibility to individual players. Closer to our work is [Mu et al., 2025], which uses concurrent stochastic games to reason about causal active and causal passive responsibilities, building on [Parker et al., 2023]. Their approach considers how strategies contribute to or prevent outcomes but does not employ counterfactual reasoning nor guarantee fair attribution. From temporal-logic perspective, [De Giacomo et al., 2025] uses LTLf to represent outcomes—similar to ours—but focuses on a single agent from a first-person perspective, whereas our approach adopts a third-person, multi-agent view. Other related works include Naumov and Tao’s logical frameworks for blameworthiness and “seeing-to-it” responsibility [Naumov and Tao, 2019; Naumov and Tao, 2021], as well as STIT-based accounts [Ciuni and Mastop, 2009; De Lima et al., 2010; Baltag et al., 2021; Abarca and Broersen, 2022]. These differ from ours in both their underlying models and their conceptions of responsibility. In particular, STIT approaches define responsibility via an agent’s modal ability to “see to it that” an outcome occurs, whereas our framework evaluates responsibility through counterfactual dependence—what would happen were an agent to act differently—and seeks a fair quantitative attribution across agents.\n2 Preliminaries\n2.1 Concurrent stochastic game\nDefinition 1. A concurrent stochastic multi-player game (CSG) is a tuple G = (Ag, S, s0, (Acti)i∈Ag, δ, Ap, L) where:\n• Ag = {1, . . . , n} is a finite set of agents;\n• S is a finite non-empty set of states;\n• s0 ∈ S is the initial state;\n• Acti is a finite set of actions for i. With each agent i and state s ∈ S, we associate a non-empty set Acti(s) of available actions that i can perform in s. Write ActAg = Act1 × · · · × Actn.\n• ∆ : S × ActAg → Dist(S) is a probabilistic transition function;\n• Ap is a finite set of atomic propositions;\n• L : S → 2Ap is the labelling function mapping each state to a set of atomic propositions drawn from Ap.\nFollowing [Kwiatkowska et al., 2021], we augment CSGs with reward structures of the form r = (rs, ra), where rs :\nS → R is the state reward and ra : ActAg → R is the action reward, and consider cumulative rewards, that is the sum of payoffs accumulated during the run until a specific point.\n1Some parameter assignments cannot be expressed as individual strategies; see [Stan et al., 2024, Remark 8]. We address this by imposing additional constraints in our parametric model (Section 6).\nProceedings of the Thirty-Fifth International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI-26)\n289\n首先,我们引入一个基于 Shapley 值 [Shapley、 1953] 的形式化框架,并证明所得分配满足公平性(责任反映贡 献)和一致性(当智能体的影响增加时,其份额不会减少) 等理想性质。尽管我们不声称这是对责任的确定性解释, 但该框架具有可解释性,并且基于一个在多个学科中广泛 使用的概念。我们的重点不是形式化道德责任,这是一个 极其复杂且尚无明确共识的领域;尽管如此,我们相信我 们的方法可以为这样一种形式化作出贡献。\n其次,我们研究智能体采用随机化无记忆策略(又称 行为策略 [Cristau 等, 2010])这一设定下的策略推理, 即从状态映射到动作上的概率分布的函数。遵循多智能体 系统(MAS)形式化方法的传统,我们用累积奖励扩展逻 辑 PATL(类似于 rPATL [Kwiatkowska 等, 2019]), 并引入责任程度算子。我们证明,该逻辑的模型检查仍属 于 PSPACE,因此并不比 rPATL[Kwiatkowska 等, 2021] 更难。最后,我们提出一种计算 Nash 均衡策略剖 面的方法,其中智能体的效用函数同时纳入奖励与责任分 配。我们证明,当智能体的时序目标被指定为可达性或安 全性条件时,这些均衡也可以在 PSPACE 中计算。由于篇 幅限制,包含完整证明的论文扩展版见 [Mu 和 Najib,\n2026]。\n相关工作 关于责任与归因的研究涵盖了大量工作;在此, 我们重点介绍一些相关研究以及关键区别。责任归因与因 果性和反事实推理密切相关。Halpern 和 Pearl [ Halpern and Pearl, 2005]的基础性工作引入了结构因果 模型,该模型为因果性与反事实依赖的形式化定义奠定了 基础。在此基础上, [Chockler 和 Halpern, 2004] 提 出了责任的定量概念,后来这一概念被扩展为责备归因的 概念 [Halpern and Kleiman‐Weiner,2018]。尽管在概 念上与我们的方法相关,但他们的框架依赖于有向无环图, 主要适用于关于信念、意图和认知状态的推理,而我们的 工作关注策略场景中的时间扩展性质。\nShapley 值也被用于责任分配。 [Friedenberg 和 Halpern, 2019] 扩展了结构模型方法 [Halpern 和 Kleiman‐Weiner,2018] 通过引入 Shapley 值来分摊责 任,并证明该分配方案满足理想性质。如前所述,他们的 模型与我们的模型不同,适用于不同的目的。类似地,[ Yazdanpanah 等, 2019] 将基于 Shapley 的责任分配应 用于并发认知博弈,从而支持认知建模,但基于确定性假 设,与我们的概率设定形成对比。 [Baier 等,2021a]研 究了扩展式博弈中的后向与前向责任,而 [Baier 等, 2021b] 采用了参数化马尔可夫链(pMCs),这与我们的 模型密切相关。无记忆策略剖面自然对应\npMC 中的参数赋值,尽管反之并不总是成立1。此外, [ Baier 等,2021b] 主要关注将变化归因于参数,其核心关 切是如何将时序满足度的变化归因于参数赋值的变化。相 比之下,我们的工作将责任分配给各个玩家。\n与我们的工作更接近的是 [Mu et al., 2025], ,其使用并发 随机博弈来推理因果主动责任和因果被动责任,并建立在 [ Parker et al.,2023] 的基础上。他们的方法考虑了策略如何促成 或阻止结果,但没有采用反事实推理,也不能保证公平归因。从 时序逻辑的视角来看, [De Giacomo et al., 2025] 使用 LTLf 来表示结果——这与我们的做法类似——但其关注的是第一人称视 角下的单个智能体,而我们的方法采用第三人称、多智能体视角。 其他相关工作包括 Naumov 和 Tao 关于可责备性与“确保” (seeing‐to‐it)责任的逻辑框架 [Naumov and Tao, 2019; Naumov and Tao, 2021], ,以及基于 STIT 的方法 [Ciuni and Mastop, 2009; De Lima et al., 2010; Baltag et al., 2021; Abarca and Broersen, 2022]。这些工作在其底层模型和对责任 的理解上都与我们的工作不同。特别是,STIT 方法通过智能体 “确保”一个⋯⋯的模态能力来定义责任\n结果发生时,而我们的框架通过反事实依赖性——即如果 某个智能体采取不同行动会发生什么——来评估责任,并 寻求在多个智能体之间进行公平的定量归因。\n2 预备知识\n2.1 并发随机博弈\n定义 1. 并发随机多玩家博弈(CSG)是一个元组 G = (\nAg S, s0, (Acti)i∈Ag, δ, Ap L),其中:\n• Ag = {1, . . . , n} 是有限智能体集合;\n• S 是一个有限非空状态集合;\n• s0 ∈ S 是初始状态;\n• Acti 是 i的有限动作集合。对于每个智能体 i 和状态 s ∈ S, 我们将一个非空集合 Acti(s) 关联起来,该集合由 i 在 s 中可 执行的可用动作组成。记作 ActAg =Act1 × · · · × Actn。\n• ∆ : S ×ActAg → Dist(S) 是一个概率转移函数;\n• Ap 是一个有限的原子命题集合;\n• L : S → 2Ap 是标签函数,它将每个状态映射到从 Ap 中选取的一组原子命题。\n参照 [Kwiatkowska 等人, 2021], 我们用形式为 r = (rs, ra) 的奖励结构来扩展 CSGs,其中 rs :S → R 是 状态奖励, ra : ActAg → R 是动作奖励;并考虑累积奖励, 即在运行过程中截至某一特定时点所累积的收益总和。\n1某些参数赋值无法表示为个体策略;参见 [Stan 等,2024,注 8]。我们通过在参数化模型中施加额外约束来解决这一问题(第 6 节)。\n第三十五届国际人工智能联合会议(IJCAI‐26)论文集\n289\n\n\n s0\ns1 s2\nb ̄1, b ̄2\nb ̄1, b2 b1, b ̄2 b1, b2\n1\n0.6 0.4 0.2 0.8\n0.12\n0.88\n∗, ∗ ∗, ∗\nFigure 1: CSG model from the running example.\nExample 2. From the running example, we construct a model in Figure 1. With Ag = {1, 2}, representing A1 and A2.\nActi = {bi, b ̄i} representing agent i ∈ Ag choosing to brake and not brake. s0 is the initial state, and L(s1) = {crash}, L(s0) = {init}, L(s2) = {pass}. Note that from s1 and s2, there are only self-loops.\nDefinition 2. A path π is a non-empty sequence s0⃗a0s1⃗a1 . . . of states and joint actions, where ⃗ai ∈ ActAg is the ith joint action, and ∆(si, ⃗ai, si+1) > 0. A history ρ is a finite path,\nρs(i) denotes the ith state of ρ, and ρ⃗a(i) denotes the ith joint\naction of ρ. In this case, we may write ρs(i) ρ⃗a(i)\n−→ ρs(i + 1). Let HistG(s) denote the set of histories of G starting from\nstate s. We write HistG(s)≤k to denote the set of histories up to and including ρs(k).\nIn this work, we assume that players have memoryless strategies. Informally, a memoryless strategy for player i prescribes, from each state s, the probability of each action a ∈ Acti(s) being chosen.\nDefinition 3. A (memoryless) strategy for i is a function from the set of states to a probability distribution over agent’s set of actions σi : S → Dist(Acti). A strategy profile is a tuple of strategies for a set of agents, it is denoted by ⃗σ = (σ1, σ2, . . . , σn). For a set (or coalition) of agents J ⊆ Ag, write ⃗σJ for (σi)i∈J .\nNote that a strategy profile ⃗σ for a game G resolves nondeterminism in the game arena. Denote by G⃗σ the resulting game arena (essentially, a Markov chain) after applying ⃗σ.\nDefinition 4. A state s and a strategy profile ⃗σ induce a set of histories s0⃗a0s1⃗a1 . . . , with P(⃗ai) · ∆(si, ⃗ai, si+1) > 0 and\ns = s0, denoted by Hist⃗σ(s). For a history ρ ∈ Hist⃗σ(s)≤k,\nthe probability of ρ = s0\n⃗a0\n−→ s1 . . . ⃗ak−1\n−→ sk, with s0 = s, is given by:\nP⃗σ(ρ) ≜\nk−1\nY\nj=0\n\"n Y\ni=1\nσi (sj )(⃗aj\ni)\n!\n· ∆(sj , ⃗aj , sj+1)\n#\n.\nFor a subset of agents J ⊆ Ag and strategies ⃗σJ , we say that a history ρ is compatible with ⃗σJ if for every k ∈ N, there\nexists a joint action ⃗ak with σi(ρs(k))(⃗ak\ni ) > 0 for each i ∈\nJ , such that ∆(sk, ⃗ak, sk+1) > 0. We denote by Hist⃗σJ (s) the set of histories starting from s and compatible with ⃗σJ that can be induced by memoryless strategies.\nDefinition 5. The payoff function defined as a map from a set of histories to a real value ℘ : HistG(s) → R|Ag|. ℘i denotes the payoff function of i ∈ Ag and is defined as:\n℘i(ρ) ≜\n\n\nt−1\nX\nj=0\n(ri\na(⃗aj ) + ri\ns(sj )) · ∆(sj , ⃗aj , sj+1)\n\n\nwhere ρ = s0\n⃗a0\n−→ s1\n⃗a1\n−→ . . . ⃗at−1\n−→ st ∈ HistG(s0).\n2.2 PATL\nPATL [Chen and Lu, 2007] is a logic for reasoning about the strategic abilities of (coalitions of) agents in probabilistic systems. In this paper, we consider PATL with bounded temporal properties.\nDefinition 6 (Syntax of PATL). The state formulae (φ) and path formulae (ψ) are defined as follows:\nφ ::= a || ¬φ || φ ∧ φ || ⟨A⟩P▷◁p[ψ] ψ ::= ⃝φ || (φU≤kφ)\nwhere a ∈ Ap is an atomic proposition, A ⊆ Ag is a set of agents, ▷◁ ∈ {≤, <, ≥, >}, p ∈ [0, 1], k ∈ N is a time bound, and ⟨A⟩ is the strategy quantifier.\nThe until operator U allows one to derive the temporal modalities ♢ (“eventually”) and □ (“always”): ♢≤kψ ≜ true U≤k ψ and □≤kψ ≜ ¬♢≤k(¬ψ).\nDefinition 7 (Semantics of PATL). The satisfaction relation for a CSG G, state s ∈ S, history ρ, atom a ∈ Ap, and PATL formula is defined as follows:\n• s |= a iff a ∈ L(s).\n• s |= ¬φ iff s ̸|= φ.\n• s |= φ ∧ φ′ iff s |= φ and s |= φ′.\n• s |= ⟨A⟩P▷◁p[ψ] iff ∃⃗σA.∀⃗σ−A.(P({ρ ∈ Hist(⃗σA,⃗σ−A)(s) | ρ |= ψ}) ▷◁ p).\n• ρ |= ⃝φ iff ρs(1) |= φ.\n• ρ |= φU≤kφ′ iff there exists i ≤ k such that: ρs(i) |= φ′, and ρs(j) |= φ for all j < i.\nWe say that formula φ is true in a CGS G iff s0 |= φ.\n3 Formalising Counterfactual Responsibility Attribution\nWe build on the concept of Necessary Element of a Sufficient Set (NESS) test [Wright, 1985], which has been used in many game-theoretic frameworks (e.g., [Braham and van Hees, 2012; Baltag et al., 2021; Parker et al., 2023]), and adapt it to our setting.\nDefinition 8. Given a CSG G, we say that agent i bears backward Counterfactual Responsibility (bCR) in a strategy profile ⃗σ for outcome φ, specified in a PATL path formula, written as i ∼ bCR⃗σ\nφ, if the following conditions hold: ∃J ⊆ Ag \\ {i} such that ∃ρ′ ∈ Hist⃗σJ (s0).ρ′ ̸|= φ and\n∀ρ′′ 