( ¬ψ)。\n定义 7(PATL 的语义)。对于 CSG G、状态 s ∈ S、历 史 ρ、原子 a ∈ Ap 以及 PATL 公式,满足关系定义如下:\n• s |= a iff a ∈ L(s).\n• s |= ¬φ iff s ̸|= φ.\n• s |= φ ∧ φ′ iff s |= φ and s |= φ′.\n• s |= ⟨A⟩P▷◁p[ψ] iff ∃⃗σA.∀⃗σ−A.(P({ρ ∈ Hist(⃗σA,⃗σ−A)(s) | ρ |= ψ}) ▷◁ p).\n• ρ |= ⃝φ iff ρs(1) |= φ.\n• ρ |= φU≤kφ′ iff there exists i ≤ k such that: ρs(i) |= φ′, and ρs(j) |= φ for all j < i.\n我们说公式 φ 在 CGS G 中为真,当且仅当 s |= φ。\n0\n3 形式化反事实责任归因\n我们基于充分集的必要要素(Necessary Element of a Sufficient Set,NESS)检验 [Wright, 1985], 这一概念,该概念已在许多博弈 论框架中使用(例如, [Braham and van Hees, 2012; Baltag et al., 2021; Parker et al., 2023]),并将其适配到我们的设定中。\n定义 8。给定一个 CSG G,我们说智能体 i 在策略剖面 ⃗σ 中对结果 φ 承担后向反事实责任(bCR),该责任由某个 PATL 路径公式⃗指定,记作 i ∼ bCRσ\nφ,若以下条件成立:\n∃J ⊆ Ag \\ {i} 使得 ∃ρ′ ∈ Hist⃗σJ(s0).ρ′ ̸|= φ 且 ∀ρ′′ ∈\nHist⃗σJ∪{i}(s0).ρ′′ |= φ。\n第三十五届国际人工智能联合会议(IJCAI‐26)论文集\n290\n\n\n In words, agent i is responsible for an outcome φ under strategy profile ⃗σ, if it belongs to some coalition J ∪ {i} whose joint strategies are sufficient to guarantee φ, while the strategies of J (without i) are not. In the definition above, bCR is a qualitative notion: i either bears responsibility or does not. Our aim is to extend this to a quantitative setting by determining “how much” responsibility agent i bears. Intuitively, this corresponds to measuring i’s marginal contribution to increasing the likelihood/probability of satisfying φ. To this end, we allocate responsibility using the Shapley value. A challenge arises when measuring the marginal contribution of a coalition that does not contain all agents (i.e., non-grand coalition) since the strategies of agents outside that coalition are undefined. We address this by assuming that φ is an undesirable outcome that agents (retrospectively) would prefer to avoid. When computing computing marginal contributions, we assume that agents outside the coalition minimise the probability of satisfying φ. This imposes no moral duty on them; it simply provides a counterfactual baseline for non-grand coalitions, whose outside behaviour would otherwise be undefined.\nDefinition 9. Given a bound2 k ∈ N, the degree of i bearing bCR in ⃗σ for φ is defined as:\nDi(⃗σ, φ) =\nX\nJ ⊆Ag\\{i}\n|J|!(|Ag| − |J| − 1)!\n|Ag|! ·\n(v⃗σ,φ(J ∪ {i}) − v⃗σ,φ(J ))\nFor any A ⊆ Ag, the function v⃗σ,φ(A) for the k-bounded histories is defined as:\nv⃗σ,φ(A) = min\n⃗σ−A\nProb({ρ | ρ ∈ Hist(⃗σA,⃗σ−A)(s0)≤k∧ρ |= φ})\nExample 3. Continuing the running example, suppose given an outcome φ := ⃝crash (i.e., crash in the next time-step), and consider the strategy profile ⃗σ corresponding to each agent choosing not to brake with probability of 1. In this case, we obtain:\nv⃗σ,φ({1, 2}) = 1, v⃗σ,φ({1}) = 0.6\nv⃗σ,φ({2}) = 0.2, v⃗σ,φ(∅) = 0.12\nTherefore:\nD1(⃗σ, φ) = 1\n2 · (0.6 − 0.12) + 1\n2 · (1 − 0.2) = 0.64\nD2(⃗σ, φ) = 1\n2 · (0.2 − 0.12) + 1\n2 · (1 − 0.6) = 0.24\nNote that A1 has a higher degree of responsibility than A2, intuitively, because A1 has a greater chance of being able to stop (i.e., 0.8, compared to 0.4 for A2). For com\nparison, under the other pure profiles (b1, b2), (b1, b ̄2), and\n(b ̄1, b2), the corresponding responsibility pairs (D1, D2) are (0, 0), (0, 0.08), and (0.48, 0), respectively.\n2This bound comes from the PATL path formula φ.\n4 Desirable Properties\nIn this section, we demonstrate the desirable properties of our responsibility attribution: efficiency, symmetry, null/dummy player, additivity, and monotonicity.\nTo formalise this, we first introduce the notion of attributable value, which represents the total amount of responsibility available for distribution among agents. The attributable value for a strategy profile is the difference between the probability of φ occurring under the strategy profile and the minimum probability achievable if agents act optimally (together) to prevent φ. To illustrate, consider an autonomous vehicle agent i approaching a T-junction. It can turn left or right. Turning left guarantees arriving late (probability 1), while turning right carries a 0.2 probability of lateness. Here, lateness is inevitable with at least a 0.2 probability. Suppose the vehicle adopts a strategy profile of turning left with certainty (probability 1). The attributable value is 1 − 0.2 = 0.8, meaning the maximum responsibility assignable to i is 0.8.\nDefinition 10. Given a strategy profile ⃗σ, an outcome φ, and a bound k ∈ N, the attributable value of φ w.r.t. ⃗σ is defined as: Υ(⃗σ, φ) = v⃗σ,φ(Ag) − min⃗σ Prob({ρ | ρ ∈ Hist⃗σ(s0)≤k ∧ ρ |= φ}).\nBy Definition 9, we have that:\nv⃗σ,φ(∅) = min\n⃗σ−∅\nProb({ρ | ρ ∈ Hist⃗σ(s0)≤k ∧ ρ |= φ}\nand since Ag \\ ∅ = Ag, therefore ⃗σAg = ⃗σ, and we immediately obtain the following lemma.\nLemma 1. For a strategy profile ⃗σ and outcome φ, it holds:\nΥ(⃗σ, φ) = v⃗σ,φ(Ag) − v⃗σ,φ(∅)\nExample 4. Consider Example 3, the attributable value is:\nΥ(⃗σ, φ) = v⃗σ,φ(Ag) − v⃗σ,φ(∅) = 1 − 0.12 = 0.88\nFairness The first three properties relate to fairness. Efficiency guarantees that the sum of assigned responsibility values equals the attributable value, ensuring no unassigned responsibility remains. Conversely, it also ensures that the distributed responsibility does not exceed the attributable value, preventing any agent from bearing excess responsibility. Symmetry ensures that two agents with identical contributions to an outcome receive equal degrees of responsibility. The dummy/null player property ensures that agents who do not contribute to an outcome receive zero responsibility.\nProposition 1 (Efficiency). For a strategy profile ⃗σ and outcome φ, it holds that: P\ni∈Ag Di(⃗σ, φ) = Υ(⃗σ, φ).\nExample 5. Consider again Example 3 and 4, note that:\nX\ni∈Ag\nDi(⃗σ, φ) = 0.64 + 0.24 = Υ(⃗σ, φ) = 0.88\nProposition 2 (Symmetry). For two agents i, j ∈ Ag, if:\n∀J ⊆ Ag \\ {i, j}, v⃗σ,φ(J ∪ {i}) = v⃗σ,φ(J ∪ {j})\nthen:\nDi(⃗σ, φ) = Dj(⃗σ, φ)\nProposition 3 (Null Player). If for all J ⊆ Ag\\{i} : v⃗σ,φ(J ∪ {i}) = v⃗σ,φ(J ), then Di(⃗σ, φ) = 0.\nProceedings of the Thirty-Fifth International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI-26)\n291\n换言之,智能体 i 在策略剖面 ⃗σ 下对某个结果 φ 负责,如果它属于某个联盟 J ∪ {i},该联盟的联合策略 足以保证 φ,而 J (不含 i)的策略却不能。\n在上文定义中,bCR 是一个定性概念: i 要么承担责 任,要么不承担责任。我们的目标是通过确定智能体 i 承 担“多少”责任,将这一概念扩展到定量情境中。直观上, 这对应于衡量 i对提高满足 φ 的可能性/概率所作的边际贡 献。为此,我们使用 Shapley 值来分配责任。当衡量一个 不包含所有智能体的联盟(即非大联盟)的边际贡献时, 会出现一个挑战,因为该联盟之外智能体的策略未定义。 我们通过假设 φ 是智能体(事后)希望避免的不合意结果 来解决这一问题。在计算边际贡献时,我们假设联盟之外 的智能体会最小化满足 φ的概率。这并未赋予它们任何道 德义务;它只是为非大联盟提供一个反事实基线,否则这 些联盟的外部行为将无法定义。\n定义 9。给定一个界2 k ∈ N, i 在 ⃗σ 中针对 φ 承载 bCR 的 程度定义如下:\nDi(⃗σ, φ) =\nX\nJ ⊆Ag\\{i}\n|J|!(|Ag| − |J| − 1)!\n|Ag|! ·\n(v⃗σ,φ(J ∪ {i}) − v⃗σ,φ(J ))\n对于任意 A ⊆ Ag,针对 k有界历史的函数 v⃗σ,φ(A) 定义如下:\nv⃗σ,φ(A) = min\n⃗σ−A\nProb({ρ | ρ ∈ Hist(⃗σA,⃗σ−A)(s0)≤k∧ρ |= φ})\n例 3。继续前面的示例,假设给定结果 φ := ⃝碰撞(即, 在下一个时间步发生碰撞),并考虑策略组合 ⃗σ ,其中每 个智能体以概率 1 选择不刹车。在这种情况下,我们得到:\nv⃗σ,φ({1, 2}) = 1, v⃗σ,φ({1}) = 0.6\nv⃗σ,φ({2}) = 0.2, v⃗σ,φ(∅) = 0.12\n因此:\nD1(⃗σ, φ) = 1\n2 · (0.6 − 0.12) + 1\n2 · (1 − 0.2) = 0.64\nD2(⃗σ, φ) = 1\n2 · (0.2 − 0.12) + 1\n2 · (1 − 0.6) = 0.24\n注意, A1 的责任程度高于A2,直观上是因为 A1 能 够阻止的机会更大(即 0.8,而 A2为 0.4)。作为 ̄对比, 在其他纯策略组合(b1, b2)、(b1, b2)和(b ̄1, b2)下,对 应的责任对(D1, D2)分别为 (0, 0)、(0, 0.08) 和 ( 0.48,0)。\n2该界来自 PATL 路径公式 φ。\n4 理想性质\n在本节中,我们展示了我们的责任归因所具有的理想性质: 效率性、对称性、零/哑玩家性、可加性和单调性。\n为了形式化这一点,我们首先引入可归因值的概念, 它表示可供在智能体之间分配的责任总量。一个策略组合 的可归因值是:在该策略组合下 φ 发生的概率,与如果智 能体(共同)以最优方式行动以阻止 φ时所能达到的最小 概率之间的差值。\n为了说明这一点,考虑一个自动驾驶车辆智能体 i 正 在接近一个 T 型路口。它可以左转或右转。左转保证会晚 到(概率为 1),而右转则有 0.2 的晚到概率。在这里, 晚到至少有 0.2 的概率不可避免。假设该车辆采用以确定 性(概率为 1)左转的策略组合。可归因值为 1 − 0.2 = 0.8, 这意味着可分配给 i 的最大责任为 0.8。\n定义 10。给定一个策略组合 ⃗σ、一个结果 φ 和一个界 k ∈ N, φ 相对于 ⃗σ 的可归因值定义为: Υ(⃗σ, φ) = v⃗σ, φ(Ag) − min⃗σ Prob({ρ | ρ ∈Hist⃗σ(s0)≤k ∧ ρ |= φ})。\n根据定义 9,可得:\nv⃗σ,φ(∅) = min\n⃗σ−∅\nProb({ρ | ρ ∈ Hist⃗σ(s0)≤k ∧ ρ |= φ}\n并且由于 Ag \\ ∅ = Ag,因此 ⃗σAg = ⃗σ,我们立即得到如下\n引理。\n引理 1。对于策略组合 ⃗σ 和结果 φ,有:\nΥ(⃗σ, φ) = v⃗σ,φ(Ag) − v⃗σ,φ(∅)\n例 4。考虑例 3,可归因值为:\nΥ(⃗σ, φ) = v⃗σ,φ(Ag) − v⃗σ,φ(∅) = 1 − 0.12 = 0.88\n公平性。前三个性质均与公平性有关。效率性保证所 分配责任值之和等于可归因值,从而确保不会留下未分配 的责任。反过来,它还确保所分配的责任不超过可归因值, 从而防止任何智能体承担过多责任。对称性确保对某一结 果作出相同贡献的两个智能体获得相同程度的责任。哑元/ 零贡献参与者性质确保未对某一结果作出贡献的智能体获 得零责任。\n命题 1(效率性)。对于策略组合 ⃗σ 和结果 φ,有:\nP\ni∈\nAg Di(⃗σ, φ) = Υ(⃗σ, φ).\n例5。再次考虑例3和例4,注意:\nX\ni∈Ag\nDi(⃗σ, φ) = 0.64 + 0.24 = Υ(⃗σ, φ) = 0.88\n命题2(对称性)。对于两个智能体 i, j ∈ Ag,如果:\n∀J ⊆ Ag \\ {i, j}, v⃗σ,φ(J ∪ {i}) = v⃗σ,φ(J ∪ {j})\n那么:\nDi(⃗σ, φ) = Dj(⃗σ, φ)\n命题 3(零玩家)。如果对所有 J ⊆ Ag\\{i} :v⃗σ,φ(J ∪{i}) = v⃗σ,φ(J),则 Di(⃗σ, φ) = 0。\n第三十五届国际人工智能联合会议(IJCAI‐26)论文集\n291\n\n\n Consistency Additivity property states that for two independent games, the Shapley value of the combined game is equal to the sum of individual values. However, this raises the question of how to combine the games and to what extent they are truly independent3, particularly, in our setting. One could define this by considering two separate CSGs, resulting in a “combined” responsibility degree that is simply the sum of the two games. However, we find this approach uninteresting and not particularly useful. Instead, we consider additivity with respect to disjunctions of outcomes φ1 ∨ φ2.\nObserve that the function Di(·, ·) is generally not additive. To see this, consider φ1 := φ and φ2 := ¬φ. Clearly, for\nany ⃗σ, Di(⃗σ, φ1 ∨ φ2) = 0, while it may be the case that\nDi(⃗σ, φ1) > 0 or Di(⃗σ, φ2) > 0. However, we can show that subadditivity holds, and for a special case of outcomes we obtain (exact) additivity. Monotonicity intuitively states that for two outcomes φ1 and φ2, where φ1 is “included” in φ2, the degree of responsibility for φ2 would likely increase. Additivity and monotonicity relate to consistency in the sense that if we expand the outcome (under certain conditions), then responsibility should increase or at least not decrease.\nProposition 4 (Sub-additivity). Given ⃗σ, for any two outcome φ1 and φ2, and any i ∈ Ag:\nDi(⃗σ, φ1 ∨ φ2) ≤ Di(⃗σ, φ1) + Di(⃗σ, φ2)\nExample 6. Continue the running example, suppose given outcomes φ1 = ⃝ crash and φ2 = ⃝ pass, consider ⃗σ corresponding to each agent choosing to brake with probability of 1, let ∨ denote φ1 ∨ φ2 for short. In this case, we have:\nv⃗σ,φ1({1, 2}) = v⃗σ,φ1({1}) = v⃗σ,φ1({2}) = v⃗σ,φ1(∅) = 0.12,\nv⃗σ,φ2 ({1, 2}) = 0.88, v⃗σ,φ2 ({1}) = 0.8,\nv⃗σ,φ2 ({2}) = 0.4, v⃗σ,φ2 (∅) = 0,\nv⃗σ,∨({1, 2}) = v⃗σ,∨({1}) = v⃗σ,∨({2}) = v⃗σ,∨(∅) = 0.12\nThus:\nD1(⃗σ, φ1) = D2(⃗σ, φ1) = 0,\nD1(⃗σ, φ2) = 0.64, D2(⃗σ, φ2) = 0.24,\nD1(⃗σ, φ1 ∨ φ2) = D2(⃗σ, φ1 ∨ φ2) = 0\nClearly, for i = {1, 2} : Di(⃗σ, φ1 ∨ φ2) ≤ Di(⃗σ, φ1) +\nDi(⃗σ, φ2).\nTo obtain exact additivity, we define the following conditions.\nDefinition 11. For two outcomes φ1 and φ2, we say that they are disjoint and avoidable, respectively, if\ni) for all ⃗σ, we have Prob({ρ | ρ ∈ Hist⃗σ(s0)≤k ∧ ρ |= φ1 ∧ φ2}) = 0\nii) there exists ⃗σ, such that Prob({ρ | ρ ∈ Hist⃗σ(s0)ρ |= (φ1 ∨ φ2)}) < 1.\nProposition 5 (Exact Additivity). Let φ1 and φ2 be two outcome formulas such that they are disjoint and avoidable, then for any i ∈ Ag:\nDi(⃗σ, φ1 ∨ φ2) = Di(⃗σ, φ1) + Di(⃗σ, φ2)\n3There have been many attempts to relax the additivity axiom, e.g. [Young, 1985; Hart and Mas-Colell, 1989; Chun, 1989; van den Brink, 2002].\nExample 7. Note that the scenario proposed in Example 6 does not satisfy Proposition 5, as it meets assumption i) but violates assumption ii).\nProposition 6 (Monotonicity). If φ1 ⇒ φ2 and φ2 is avoidable, then for any i ∈ Ag and a given ⃗σ:\nDi(⃗σ, φ1) ≤ Di(⃗σ, φ2)\nIntuitively, responsibility should not decrease when the outcome becomes more inclusive - any trace satisfying φ1 also satisfies φ2, so agents influencing φ1 also influencing φ2, potentially to a greater extent.\nExample 8. Continue the running example, consider φ1= ⃝ crash and φ2=♢≤2 crash and ⃗σ corresponding to each agent choosing not to brake with probability of 1. Clearly, in this case, φ1 ⇒ φ2 and φ2 is avoidable. We have: D1(⃗σ, φ1) =\nD1(⃗σ, φ2) = 0.64, D2(⃗σ, φ1) = D2(⃗σ, φ2) = 0.24. We have “=” specifically here as there is only a self-loop from s1.\n5 Logic with CR attributions: PATL-SR\nWe introduce PATL-SR, a variant of PATL that incorporates quantified reward 