▷◁ d 量化。\n定义 13。给定模型 G,新 PATL-SR 算子的语义定义如下; 完整的形式化定义见补充材料。\n• s |= ⟨A⟩R▷◁q[ψ] 当且仅当存在联盟 A 的一个策略组合, 使得从状态 s 出发、与该策略一致且满足 ψ 的路径的期望\n累积奖励为 ▷◁ q,即 ∃⃗σA.E⃗σA\nG s,rew(r, ψ) ▷◁ q,其中: rew(r, ψ)(ρ) =\nPk\ni=0(ra(ρa(i)) + rs(ρs(i))) if ρ |= ψ;\n0 otherwise\n其中 k 表示......的时间界。 ψ\n• s |= ⟨A⟩D▷◁d[bCRi(⃗σ, ψ)] iff Di(⃗σA, ψ) ▷◁ d, where: Di(⃗σA, ψ) = P\nJ ⊆A\\{i}\n|J |!(|A|−|J |−1)!\n|A|! · (v⃗σA,ψ(J ∪\n{i}) − v⃗σA,ψ(J )).\n示例9。考虑我们的运行示例,其结果为“在2个时间步内发生 碰撞”,可以表示为: ψ = ⟨{1, 2}⟩♢≤2 crash。假设1和2均 选择在......下刹车\n⃗σ,因此:v⃗σ,ψ({1, 2}) =v⃗σ,ψ({1}) = v⃗σ,ψ({2}) = v⃗σ,ψ(∅) = 0.12, 并且因此 D1(⃗σ, ψ) = D2(⃗σ, ψ) = 0。所以:D≤0[bCRi(⃗σ, ψ)] 为真。\n第三十五届国际人工智能联合会议(IJCAI‐26)论文集\n292\n\n\n The model checking of PATL-SR works in a similar way to rPATL proposed in [Kwiatkowska et al., 2019]. The main difference is that we have the new D▷◁d[bCRi(⃗σ, ψ)] opera\ntor, which corresponds to computing the function Di(⃗σA, ψ) in Definition 13. Computing such function can be done in polynomial space.\nLemma 2. Given a CSG G, a coalition A, strategy profile ⃗σ, and a path formula ψ, computing Di(⃗σA, ψ) can be done in polynomial space.\nTheorem 1. Model checking PATL-SR is in PSPACE.\n6 CR-aware Strategic Decision-Making\nThis section studies how agents choose strategies when they are aware of counterfactual responsibility attributions. We define a utility function that combines an agent’s payoff with its degree of responsibility, and use Nash equilibrium (NE) to characterise stable strategy profiles under this utility. To retain decidability, we restrict attention to memoryless strategies and reachability/safety objectives [Ummels and Wojtczak, 2011]. First, we introduce a parametric model. Observe that the set of all memoryless strategies for player i from state s can be encoded by a set of variables V i\ns = {xa : a ∈ Acti(s)}.\nIntuitively, the value of xa ∈ V i\ns corresponds to the probability of action a being chosen by player i in state s. Let\nVi = S\ns∈S V i\ns . A memoryless (mixed) strategy for i in G thus corresponds to an evaluation of such a set of variables represented by a function Ci : V i → R.\nDefinition 14. Given G = (Ag, S, s0, (Acti)i∈Ag, δ, Ap, L), we construct the corresponding parametric stochastic multi-agent system (PSMAS) as a tuple M=(Ag, S, s0, Act, V, ∆, Ap, L), where: V i = {xi,1, . . . , xi,k} ⊆ V is a finite set of variables (parameters) over Rk for each agent i; ∆ : S × ActAg × S → Fx is the probabilistic transition function, where Fx is the set of polynomials over V with rational coefficients which can be viewed as a parametric transition probability matrix that respects the distribution in δ.\nObserve that the resulting PSMAS M is polynomial in size wrt the game G. Next, we introduce the notion of admissible evaluations in a given PSMAS. An evaluation Ci is admissible if: (1) ∆Ci (s, ⃗a, s) ∈ [0, 1] for all s, s′ ∈ S and ⃗a ∈ ActAg, i.e., each joint action has a probability between 0 and 1; (2) Ci(x) ∈ [0, 1] for all i ∈ Ag and x ∈ V i, i.e., for each player, each action probability is between 0 and 1; (3) P\nx∈V i\ns Ci(x) = 1 for all i ∈ Ag and s ∈ S, i.e., For each player and each state, the total of action probability values should equal to 1. Henceforth, we assume that the model takes the form of a PSMAS. When evaluating solutions, we further assume that such solutions are admissible.\nDefinition 15 (Responsibility-aware Utility Function). Given M and an outcome φ and strategy profile ⃗σ, for an agent i, the utility function is defined as:\nuφ\ni (⃗σ) ≜\nX\nρ∈Hist⃗σ (s0)\n℘i(ρ) − λ · Di(⃗σ, φ) (1)\nwhere ℘i(ρ) and Di(⃗σ, φ) are the payoff function and bCR degree function defined in Definition 5 and 9 respectively, λ ∈ R is a coefficient.\nDefinition 16 (NE-COMPUTATION). Given a PSMAS M, a\nPATL-SR path formula φ, and utility functions (uφ\ni (⃗σ))i∈Ag, the NE-COMPUTATION problem is to compute a NE strategy profile ⃗σ that satisfies φ.\nThe high level process to address NE-COMPUTATION consists of two main steps: (i) Satisfaction filtering: identify the set of strategy profiles that satisfy φ. This is achieved through parametric model checking on M, which yields rational valuation functions V i for each agent i ∈ Ag. These functions capture the probabilities of selecting actions a ∈ Act in each state s under mixed strategies. (ii) Equilibrium filtering: refine this set by isolating the strategies that constitute an NE. This involves formulating a system of equations characterising NE conditions and solving them within the strategy set obtained in (i). Proposition 7 (Quasi-Concavity of Utility). The utility func\ntion uφ\ni is quasi-concave if the following conditions hold: 1) linearity of reward: the expected reward is linear in ⃗σi;\n2) linearity of responsibility contribution: the responsibility-valuation function v⃗σ,φ(A) is linear in ⃗σi for all coalition A ⊆ Ag;\n3) fixed strategies of others: the strategy profile ⃗σ−i of other agents is fixed.\nIn our setting, quasi-concavity is evaluated with respect to an individual agent’s mixed strategy. To ensure this is welldefined, we fix the strategies of all other agents. This is a standard approach in both game-theoretic best-response analysis and parametric model checking [Shoham and Leyton-Brown, 2009; Basset et al., 2015]. When both reward and responsibility allocation are linear in the agent’s strategy, the resulting utility function is quasi-concave, enabling NE computation via convex optimisation. In a PSMAS, memoryless strategies are encoded symbolically by parameters denoting action probabilities. With the other agents’ strategies fixed, quasi-concavity follows whenever the reward and responsibility terms are linear in the strategy parameters of the agent under consideration. We show that, for reachability and safety objectives in our setting, these terms satisfy the required conditions, yielding the following result.\nProposition 8. In a game where the formula φ is a reachability or safety formula, the quasi-concavity property (Proposition 7) holds. In general, uφ\ni is quasi-concave in ⃗σi when the agent’s preference for mixing strategies leads to at least as good an outcome as playing pure strategies, or formally, when the level\nsets ⃗σi | uφ\ni (⃗σi, ⃗σ−i) ≥ c are convex for every c ∈ R [Os\nborne and Rubinstein, 1994]. This result enables the use of convex optimisation (best response over pure strategies) to compute Nash equilibria when the assumptions hold.\nComputing bCR-aware utility. For a fixed outcome φ,\ncomputing uφ\ni (⃗σ) requires: i) calculating the expected re\nwards: E(℘i({ρ ∈ Hist⃗σA (s) | ρ |=M ψ})), and ii) cal\nculating the bCR degree: Di(⃗σA, ψ). Finally, computing the\nProceedings of the Thirty-Fifth International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI-26)\n293\nPATL‐SR 的模型检测工作方式与 [Kwiatkowska 等 人, 2019] 中提出的 rPATL 类似。主要区别在于,我们有 一个新的 D▷◁d[bCRi(⃗σ, ψ)] 算子,它对应于计算定义 13 中 的函数 Di(⃗σA, ψ)。这种函数的计算可以在多项式空间中完\n成。\n引理 2. 给定一个 CSG G、一个联盟 A、一个策略组合 ⃗σ 以及 一个路径公式 ψ,计算 Di(⃗σA, ψ) 可在多项式空间中完成。\n定理 1. PATL-SR 的模型检测属于 PSPACE。\n6 CR 感知的战略决策\n本节研究智能体在意识到反事实责任归因时如何选择策略。 我们定义了一个将智能体收益与其责任程度相结合的效用 函数,并使用纳什均衡(NE)来刻画该效用下的稳定策略 组合。为保持可判定性,我们将注意力限制在无记忆策略 和可达性/安全性目标上 [Ummels 和 Wojtczak, 2011]。\n首先,我们引入一个参数化模型。注意,玩家 i 从状态 s 出发的所有无记忆策略集合可以由一组变量 V i\ns = {xa 编 码: a ∈ Acti(s)}。直观上, xa ∈ V i\ns 的值对应于玩家 i 在 状态 s 中选择动作 a 的概率。令 V i = S\ns∈S V i\ns。因此, i 在 G 中的无记忆(混合)策略对应于对这样一组变量的一种 赋值,该赋值由函数 Ci : V i → R 表示。\n定义 14. 给定 G = (Ag S, s0, (Acti)i∈Ag, δ, Ap L),我们构 造相应的参数化随机多智能体系统(PSMAS)为元组M= (Ag S, s0, Act V , ∆, Ap L),其中:V i =\n{xi,1, . . . , xi,k} ⊆ V 是每个智能体 i 在 Rk 上的有限变量\n(参数)集合; ∆ : S ×ActAg × S → Fx 是概率转移函 数,其中 Fx 是 V 上具有有理系数的多项式集合,可视为 一个参数化转移概率矩阵,它遵循 δ中的分布。\n注意,所得的 PSMAS M 的规模相对于博弈 G 是多项 式的。接下来,我们引入给定 PSMAS 中可容许评估的概念。 评估 Ci 是可容许的,如果:(1) 对所有 s、 s′ ∈ S 和 ⃗a ∈ ActAg,有 ∆Ci(s, ⃗a, s) ∈ [0, 1] ,即每个联合动作的概率都在 0 和 1 之间;(2) 对所有 i ∈ Ag 和 x ∈ V i,有 Ci(x) ∈ [0, 1] , 即对每个参与者,每个动作概率都在 0 和 1 之间;(3) 对所有 i ∈ Ag 和 s ∈ S,有 P\nx∈V i\ns Ci(x) = 1 ,即对每个参与者 和每个状态,动作概率值之和应等于 1。此后,我们假设模型 采取 PSMAS 的形式。在评估解时,我们进一步假设这样的解 是可容许的。\n定义 15(责任感知效用函数)。给定 M 、一个结果 φ 和策略组合 ⃗σ,对于智能体 i,效用函数定义为:\nuφ\ni (⃗σ) ≜\nX\nρ∈Hist⃗σ (s0)\n℘i(ρ) − λ · Di(⃗σ, φ) (1)\n其中, ℘i(ρ) 和 Di(⃗σ, φ) 分别是定义 5 和定义 9 中定义的 收益函数和 bCR 度函数, λ ∈R 是一个系数。\n定义 16(NE-COMPUTATION)。给定一个 PSMAS M、一个 PATL-SR 路径公式 φ以及效用函数(uφ\ni (⃗σ))i∈Ag,\nNE-COMPUTATION 问题是计算一个 NE 策略组合 ⃗σ ,使其满足 φ。\n处理 NE‐COMPUTATION 的高层流程由两个主要步 骤组成:(i)满足性筛选:确定满足 φ 的策略组合集合。 这通过对 M进行参数化模型检验来实现,从而产生有理估 值函数 V i (针对每个智能体 i ∈ Ag)。这些函数刻画了 在混合策略下,在每个状态 s 中选择动作 a ∈ Act 的概 率。(ii)均衡筛选:通过分离出构成 NE 的策略来细化该 集合。这涉及构建一个刻画 NE 条件的方程组,并在(i) 中获得的策略集合内求解该方程组。\n命题 7(效用的拟凹性)。若以下条件成立,则效用函数 uφ\ni\n是拟凹的:\n1) 奖励的线性性:期望奖励关于 ⃗σi是线性的;2) 责任贡 献的线性性:对于所有联盟 A ⊆ Ag,责任估值函数v⃗σ,φ (A)关于 ⃗σi 都是线性的;3) 其他智能体的策略固定:其他 智能体的策略配置 ⃗σ−i 是固定的。\n在我们的设定中,拟凹性是针对单个智能体的混合策 略来评估的。为确保其良定义,我们固定所有其他智能体 的策略。这在博弈论最佳响应分析和参数化模型检验中都 是标准方法 [Shoham 和 Leyton‐Brown,2009; Basset 等, 2015]。当奖励和责任分配都关于该智能体的 策略呈线性时,所得的效用函数是拟凹的,从而能够通过 凸优化计算纳什均衡(NE)。\n在 PSMAS 中,无记忆策略通过表示动作概率的参数 进行符号化编码。在其他智能体的策略固定时,只要奖励 项和责任项关于策略是线性的,就可推出拟凹性\n所考虑智能体的策略参数。我们证明,在我们的设定中, 对于可达性与安全性目标,这些项满足所需条件,从而得 到以下结果。\n命题 8. 在公式 φ 是可达性或安全性公式的博弈中,拟凹 性性质(命题 7)成立。\n一般而言,当智能体对混合策略的偏好所产生的结果至少 与采取纯策略一样好时,或者形式化地说,当水平集 ⃗σi | uφ\ni\n(⃗σi, ⃗σ−i) ≥ c 对每个 c ∈ R [ 都是凸集时, uφ\ni 关于 ⃗σi 是拟 凹的(奥斯本和鲁宾斯坦, 1994])。该结果使得在假设成立 时,可以利用凸优化(在纯策略上求最佳响应)来计算纳什均 衡。 计算 bCR 感知效用。对于固定结果 φ,计算 uφ\ni (⃗σ) 需要: i) 计算期望奖励: E(℘i({ρ ∈ Hist⃗σA(s) | ρ |=M ψ})); 以及 ii) 计算 bCR 程度: Di(⃗σA, ψ)。最后,计算\n第三十五届国际人工智能联合会议(IJCAI‐26)论文集\n293\n\n\n best response set BRi(⃗σAg\\i) corresponds to finding the set of strategies σi that maximise the utility as the NE.\nDefinition 17 (NE for bCR-aware Strategy). Given M, for each agent i, and ⃗σAg\\i, a strategy σi is a best response w.r.t. the utility function defined in (1), if it is the set:\nBRi(⃗σAg\\i) ≜{σi | mσaix\n(uφ\ni (σi, ⃗σAg\\i))}\nA strategy profile ⃗σ is a mixed NE if it is a best response for every agent, meaning that ⃗σ ∈ BRi(⃗σAg\\i) holds for all i ∈ Ag in M.\nExample 10. Revisiting our running example, consider ψ = ⃝ crash, A1 and A2 choose “braking” with probability x1 and ‘x2 respectively under ⃗σ. Suppose that the payoffs for A1 and A2 “braking” yields 3 and 1 respectively, while “notbraking” results in a payoff of 2 and 3 respectively, the payoffs of reaching s1 (crash) for A1 and A2 is −3 and −1 respectively. The expected payoff can be computed as:\nX\nρ∈Hist⃗σ (s0)\n℘1(ρ) = x1 + 0.4x2 − 1.6x1x2 − 1\nX\nρ∈Hist⃗σ (s0)\n℘2(ρ) = −1.6x1 − 2x2 + 1.6x1x2 + 2\nv⃗σ,ψ({1, 2}) = v⃗σ,ψ({2}) = 0.2x1(1 − x2)\nv⃗σ,ψ({1}) = v⃗σ,ψ(∅) = 0.12x1x2\nD1(⃗σ, ψ) = 0, D2(⃗σ, ψ) = 0.2x1 − 0.32x1x2\nConsider an example form of the polynomial function:\nX\nρ∈Hist⃗σ (s0)\n℘i(ρ) − 10 · Di(⃗σ, ψ)\nwe obtain the utility function as:\nuψ\n1 (⃗σ) = x1 + 0.4x2 − 1.6x1x2 − 1\nuψ\n2 (⃗σ) = −3.6x1 − 2x2 + 4.8x1x2 + 2.\nParametric best response expressions. When performing parametric model checking against PATL-SR formulas to evaluate best responses, we obtain parametric expressions that encode optimal strategies. These expressions include parameters representing the probabilities of different actions for each agent. By varying these parameters, we can identify best-response strategies within the strategy profile space, i.e., action probabilities that maximise each agent’s utility. This approach enables a systematic exploration of how changes in action probabilities affect both the overall performance and allocation of responsibility within the strategy profile.\nFinding the stable responsibility-aware strategy. If the\nutility function uφ\ni is quasi-concave with respect to agent i’s mixed strategies in a given model M, then a mixed strategy profile ⃗σ is a NE if and only if every pure strategy played with positive probability in σi is a best response to the mixed strategies of the other agents [Osborne and Rubinstein, 1994]. This implies that, in a mixed NE, an agent randomises only among actions that yield the same expected utility, i.e., every action in the support of an agent’s equilibrium mixed\nstrategy must lead to an identical valuation: uφ\ni (⃗σ−i, σi) =\nuφ\ni (⃗σ−i, σi′). For any two actions a, a′ ∈ Act corresponding to σi and σi′, provided they have nonzero probabilities. This property forms the basis for expressing the NE condition:\n\n\n\nuφ\ni (⃗σ−i, σi) = uφ\ni (⃗σ−i, σi′) ∀σi, σi′\nPm\nj=1 σij = 1 ∀i 0 ≤ σij ≤ 1 ∀i, j\n(2)\nSolving these equations allows us to identify the NE and determine the optimal strategy profile for the given objective. This leads to the following theorem, whose proof follows from a reduction to the existential theory of real numbers [Canny, 1988].\nTheorem 2. Computing an NE strategy satisfying PATL-SR formula φ can be done in PSPACE.\nExample 11. Revisiting Example 10, by solving the NE condition equations, we have: x1 = 0.42, x2 = 0.625.\n7 Conclusion and Future Work\nWe introduce a formal definition of backward (counterfactual) responsibility allocation and provide a framework to model and reason about it. Our definition of responsibility allocation satisfies desirable properties such as fairness and consistency. Utilising this definition, we propose PATLSR as a language to strategically reason about responsibility allocation, reward, and temporal objectives in probabilistic multi-agent systems. We demonstrate that the model checking problem is in PSPACE, thus no more complex than rPATL as proposed in [Kwiatkowska et al., 2019]. Furthermore, we present an approach to calculate NE strategy profiles, where players’ utility functions consider both reward and responsibility allocation. These strategy profiles correspond to stable behaviour in the game and offer a method to determine how agents should or would choose their strategies. This corresponds to game theory’s normative (how agents should/ought to act) and descriptive (how agents would act) interpretations [Wooldridge, 2012]. Both interpretations are valuable for the analysis, verification, and design of responsibility-aware MASs. As discussed in [Kwiatkowska et al., 2018], bounded temporal properties may require memory. Therefore, a potential future direction is to generalise our approach to finite-memory agents. However, this would necessitate a different approach, as using a parametric model would no longer suffice. Another intriguing future direction is to incorporate responsibility allocation into normative systems [  ̊Agotnes et al., 2007], where norms can be introduced to achieve desirable outcomes from the designer’s perspective [Perelli, 2019]. Additionally, when integrating learning components into the systems, such as in a multi-agent reinforcement learning setting, it is worth exploring how to incorporate responsibility allocation with rewards. This could involve designing reward structures or reward machines [Icarte et al., 2022] to achieve desirable outcomes.\nAcknowledgments\nChunyan Mu acknowledges the support provided by the University of Aberdeen through the research leave project on\nProceedings of the Thirty-Fifth International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI-26)\n294\n最佳反应集 BRi(⃗σAg\\i) 对应于寻找作为 NE 使效用最大化的策\n略集合 σi 。\n定义 17(bCR-aware 策略的 NE)。给定 M,对于每个智能体 i 以及 ⃗σAg\\i,如果策略 σi 是如下集合,那么它就是关于 (1) 中定 义的效用函数的最佳响应:\nBRi(⃗σAg\\i) ≜{σi | mσaix\n(uφ\ni (σi, ⃗σAg\\i))}\n策略组合 ⃗σ 是一个混合 NE,如果它对每个智能体都是最佳 响应,也就是说, ⃗σ ∈ BRi(⃗σAg\\i) 对所有 i ∈ Ag in M 都成立。\n例 10。重新审视我们的运行示例,考虑 ψ =⃝ 碰撞, A1 和 A2 在 ⃗σ 下分别以概率 x1 和 ‘x2 选择 “ 刹车 ”。假设 对于 A1 和 A2 ,“刹车”分别带来 3 和 1 的收益,而“不刹 车”分别带来 2 和 3 的收益;对于 A1 和 A2 ,到达 s1 (碰撞)的收益分别为 −3 和 −1 。期望收益可计算如下:\nX\nρ∈Hist⃗σ (s0)\n℘1(ρ) = x1 + 0.4x2 − 1.6x1x2 − 1\nX\nρ∈Hist⃗σ (s0)\n℘2(ρ) = −1.6x1 − 2x2 + 1.6x1x2 + 2\nv⃗σ,ψ({1, 2}) = v⃗σ,ψ({2}) = 0.2x1(1 − x2)\nv⃗σ,ψ({1}) = v⃗σ,ψ(∅) = 0.12x1x2\nD1(⃗σ, ψ) = 0, D2(⃗σ, ψ) = 0.2x1 − 0.32x1x2\n考虑多项式函数的一个示例形式:\nX\nρ∈Hist⃗σ (s0)\n℘i(ρ) − 10 · Di(⃗σ, ψ)\n我们得到效用函数为:\nuψ\n1 (⃗σ) = x1 + 0.4x2 − 1.6x1x2 − 1\nuψ\n2 (⃗σ) = −3.6x1 − 2x2 + 4.8x1x2 + 2.\n参数化最佳响应表达式。当针对 PATL‐SR 公式执行参数 化模型检查以评估最佳响应时,我们会获得编码最优策略 的参数化表达式。这些表达式包含表示每个智能体不同动 作概率的参数。通过改变这些参数,我们可以在策略组合 空间中识别最佳响应策略,即最大化每个智能体效用的动 作概率。该方法能够系统地探索动作概率的变化如何影响 策略组合中的整体性能与责任分配。\n寻找稳定的责任感知策略。如果效用函数 uφ\ni 关于智能体 i 在给定模型 M 中的混合策略是拟凹的,那么混合策略 组合 ⃗σ 是一个 NE,当且仅当在 σi 中以正概率选择的每 个纯策略都是对其他智能体 [(Osborne 和 Rubinstein, 1994])的混合策略的最佳响应。这意味着,在混合 NE 中, 智能体只会在产生相同期望效用的动作之间进行随机化, 即智能体均衡混合\n策略必须导致相同的估值: uφ\ni (⃗σ−i, σi =\nuφ\ni ⃗σ−i σi′ a a′ ∈ ) ( , )。对于任意两\n个动作,即 Act 中分别对应于 σi 和 σi′ 的动作,只要它们具有非零概率。 这一性质构成了表达 NE 条件的基础:\n\n\n\nuφ\ni (⃗σ−i, σi) = uφ\ni (⃗σ−i, σi′) ∀σi, σi′\nPm\nj=1 σij = 1 ∀i 0 ≤ σij ≤ 1 ∀i, j\n(2)\n求解这些方程使我们能够识别 NE,并确定给定目标下的 最优策略组合。由此得到以下定理,其证明可通过归约到 实数的存在理论 [Canny, 1988] 得出。\n定理 2. 计算满足 PATL-SR 公式 φ 的 NE 策略可在\nPSPACE 中完成。\n示例 11。重访示例 10,通过求解 NE 条件方程,可得:\nx1 = 0.42, x2 = 0.625。\n7 结论与未来工作\n我们给出了反向(反事实)责任分配的形式化定义,并提 供了一个用于对其建模和推理的框架。我们的责任分配定 义满足公平性和一致性等理想性质。利用该定义,我们提 出 PATL‐SR 作为一种语言,用于在概率多智能体系统中 对责任分配、奖励和时间目标进行策略性推理。我们证明 模型检查问题属于 PSPACE,因此并不比 [ Kwiatkowska 等, 2019] 中提出的 rPATL 更复杂。此外, 我们提出了一种计算 NE 策略组合的方法,其中参与者的 效用函数同时考虑奖励和责任分配。这些策略组合对应于 博弈中的稳定行为,并提供了一种确定智能体应当或将会 如何选择其策略的方法。这对应于博弈论的规范性解释 (智能体应当/应该如何行动)和描述性解释(智能体将会 如何行动)[Wooldridge, 2012]。这两种解释对于责任 感知多智能体系统(MASs)的分析、验证和设计都具有 重要价值。\n正如 [Kwiatkowska 等人所讨论的, 2018], 有界时序 性质可能需要记忆。因此,一个潜在的未来方向是将我们 的方法推广到有限记忆智能体。然而,这将需要一种不同 的方法,因为使用参数化模型将不再足够。另一个有趣的 未来方向是将责任分配纳入规范系统 [  ̊Agotnes 等人, 2007], 其中可以引入规范,以从\n设计者的视角 [Perelli, 2019]。此外,当将学习组件集成 到系统中时,例如在多智能体强化学习环境中,值得探索 如何将责任分配与奖励相结合。这可能涉及设计奖励结构 或奖励机 [Icarte 等人, 2022] 以实现期望结果。\n致谢\nChunyan Mu 感谢阿伯丁大学通过研究休假项目提供的支持, 该项目关于\n第三十五届国际人工智能联合会议(IJCAI‐26)论文集\n294\n\n\n “Responsibility-aware Trustworthy AI”, and the National Decommissioning Centre (NDC) project on “Decision-Making for Oil & Gas Decommissioning: A Formal AI-Driven Approach”.\nReferences\n[Abarca and Broersen, 2022] Aldo Iva ́n Ram ́ırez Abarca and Jan M Broersen. A stit logic of responsibility. In AAMAS, pages 1717–1719, 2022.\n[  ̊Agotnes et al., 2007] Thomas  ̊Agotnes, Wiebe van der Hoek, and Michael Wooldridge. Normative system games. In Proceedings of the 6th international joint conference on Autonomous agents and multiagent systems, pages 1–8, 2007.\n[Baier et al., 2021a] Christel Baier, Florian Fun